{"record_type": "item", "id": "84cc9d7546eb499e8cb9e3753bd10663", "response_id": "turn_4e1e9a1c61934b73b44f2449e77b5487", "type": "message", "status": "completed", "created_at": 1791075922, "role": "user", "content": [{"type": "input_text", "text": "We are searching for stable, non-toxic solar absorbers in the JARVIS-DFT dft_3d dataset (doi:10.1016/j.commatsci.2025.114063). A hit is defined as: SLME \u2265 30%, ehull \u2264 0.05 eV/atom, and no Pb/Cd/Hg/Tl/As. Cheap descriptors available per material: composition, OptB88vdW band gap, ehull, formation energy. The expensive oracle is SLME (one budget unit per material). Base rate of hits among stable non-toxic candidates: 9.47% (129 out of 1,362).\n\nPlease search the literature (OpenAlex) and answer:\n1. Which structural families or chemical compositions are known to produce high SLME or high photovoltaic efficiency? (cite DOIs)\n2. What is the relationship between OptB88vdW band gap and SLME? Is there a known optimal band gap window?\n3. Have Bayesian optimization, active learning, or other surrogate-model approaches been used to accelerate SLME screening on JARVIS data? What speedups were achieved?\n4. Are there known non-toxic, stable material families (e.g. kesterites, chalcogenides, oxides, halide perovskites without Pb) that cluster near the SLME \u2265 30% threshold?\n5. What cheap compositional or structural features best predict high SLME?\n\nReturn: key findings with DOIs, any known hit families, and the most important open gaps for our campaign."}], "created_by": "jtfernandez@berkeley.edu"}
